        "execSummary":  "The mobilization of $500B in institutional financing for AI infrastructure signals a structural shift: AI compute is no longer evaluated as a technology expense but as a capital asset with measurable yield, residual value, and investment-grade returns. For enterprise leaders building AI strategy, this has two implications: first, compute access will become increasingly financeable through capital markets rather than just cloud contracts; second, the institutionalization of AI infrastructure investment accelerates the buildout timeline, meaning enterprise AI capabilities will scale faster than current projections assume. The rising GPU rental rates ΓÇö up 38% in five months ΓÇö indicate supply-demand tension that enterprises with long-term AI roadmaps should address now through reserved capacity agreements. The open capital market framing is strategic positioning by NVIDIA to avoid antitrust scrutiny while maintaining control over the underlying hardware standard.",
